.TH PSTAT 8 "September 2, 1997"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
pstat \- print system facts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/sbin/pstat
.B \-iptfsT
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pstat
prints the contents of certain system tables.
It reads the kernel's records through
.IR sysctl (3)
and never opens
.I /dev/kmem
or a core file, so it prints no kernel address.
The inode table, the open file table and the terminal record
are returned to the super-user alone:
.BR \-i ,
.B \-f
and
.B \-t
fail with ``Operation not permitted'' for any other user,
and
.B \-T
fails the same way if the kernel withholds its capacity record.
The process table, the swap map and the table limits
need no privilege.
With no option,
.B \-f
is assumed.
Options are
.TP \w'WCHAN\ 'u
.B \-i
Print the inode table, one line for each inode with a reference,
with these headings:
.IP FLAGS
Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:
.RS
.IP L
locked
.IP U
update time
.RI ( fs (5))
must be corrected
.IP A
access time must be corrected
.IP M
file system is mounted here
.IP W
wanted by another process (L flag is on)
.IP T
contains a text file
.IP C
changed time must be corrected
.IP S
shared lock applied
.IP E
exclusive lock applied
.IP Z
someone waiting for a lock
.IP P
pipe
.IP m
modified
.IP r
being renamed
.IP x
text, but impure
.RE
.PD 0
.IP CNT
Number of open file table entries for this inode.
.IP DEVICE
Major and minor device number of file system in which
this inode resides.
.IP RDC
Reference count of shared locks on the inode.
.IP WRC
Reference count of exclusive locks on the inode (this may
be > 1 if, for example, a file descriptor is inherited across a fork).
.IP INO
I-number within the device.
.IP MODE
Mode bits, see
.IR chmod (2).
.IP NLK
Number of links to this inode.
.IP UID
User ID of owner.
.IP SIZE/DEV
Number of bytes in an ordinary file, or
major and minor device of special file.
.PD
.TP
.B \-p
Print the process table, one line for each live process,
with these headings:
.IP S
Run state encoded thus:
.RS
.IP 1
waiting for some event
.IP 3
runnable
.IP 4
being created
.IP 5
being terminated
.IP 6
stopped (by signal or  under trace)
.RE
.PD 0
.IP F
Miscellaneous state variables, or'ed together (hexadecimal):
.RS
.IP 0001 9n
loaded
.IP 0002
system process (swapper)
.IP 0004
locked for swap out
.IP 0008
swap save area
.IP 0010
traced
.IP 0020
used in tracing
.IP 0040
user settable lock in core
.IP 0100
process resulted from
.IR vfork (2)
.IP 0200
parent in vfork, waiting for child
.IP 0400
parent has released child in vfork
.IP 1000
detached inherited by init
.IP 2000
no SIGCHLD signal to parent
.IP 4000
selecting; wakeup/waiting danger
.RE
.IP PRI
Scheduling priority, see
.IR setpriority (2).
.IP UID
Effective user ID.
.IP CPU
Weighted integral of CPU time, for scheduler.
.IP NI
Nice level,
see
.IR setpriority (2).
.IP PGRP
Process number of root of process group.
.IP PID
The process ID number.
.IP PPID
The process ID of parent process.
.IP SIZE
Size of the process image (data plus stack) in bytes, hexadecimal.
.PD
.TP
.B \-t
Print the console terminal
with these headings:
.IP RAW
Number of characters in raw input queue.
.PD 0
.IP CAN
Number of characters in canonicalized input queue.
.IP OUT
Number of characters in output queue.
.IP MODE
See
.IR tty (4).
.IP DEL
Number of delimiters (newlines) in canonicalized input queue.
.IP COL
Calculated column position of terminal.
.IP STATE
Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:
.RS
.IP T
delay timeout in progress
.IP W
waiting for open to complete
.IP O
open
.IP F
outq has been flushed during DMA
.IP C
carrier is on
.IP B
busy doing output
.IP A
process is awaiting output
.IP X
open for exclusive use
.IP S
output stopped
.IP H
hangup on close
.IP b
tandem queue blocked
.IP r
collision in read select
.IP w
collision in write select
.IP a
asynchronous i/o mode
.RE
.IP PGRP
Foreground process group of the terminal.
.PD
.TP
.B \-f
Print the open file table, one line for each open entry,
with these headings:
.IP TYPE
The type of object the file table entry points to;
a number stands for an unnamed type.
.PD 0
.IP FLG
Miscellaneous state variables encoded thus:
.RS
.IP R
open for reading
.IP W
open for writing
.IP A
open for appending
.IP S
shared lock present
.IP X
exclusive lock present
.IP I
signal pgrp when data ready
.IP n
non-blocking
.IP m
marked by the collector
.IP d
deferred to the next collector pass
.RE
.IP CNT
Number of processes that know this open file.
.IP MSG
Number of messages outstanding for this file.
.IP INO
The inode number of the inode or pipe for this file,
or
.B \-
for any other type of object.
.IP OFFSET
The file offset (see
.IR lseek (2)).
.PD
.PP
.B \-s
print information about swap space usage: the number of free runs
in the swap map and the size of the map,
and the number of kilobytes of swap used and free.
.PP
.B \-T
prints the number of used slots and the limit of the file, inode
and process tables from the kernel's capacity record
and is useful for checking to see how full system tables have become if the
system is under heavy load;
it ends with the swap usage of
.BR \-s .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B pstat
exits with status 1 and prints
.RI `` "pstat: view: reason" ''
when a
.IR sysctl (3)
call fails, as in
.RB `` "pstat: files: Operation not permitted" ''
for a caller other than the super-user.
.SH SEE ALSO
iostat(1),
ps(1),
systat(1),
vmstat(1),
sysctl(3),
stat(2),
fs(5),
.br
K. Thompson,
.I UNIX Implementation
.SH BUGS
It would be very useful if the system recorded \*(lqmaximum occupancy\*(rq
on the tables reported by
.B \-T;
even more useful if these tables were dynamically allocated.
